Problem:
If $a \star b = \dfrac{\left(\dfrac{1}{b} - \dfrac{1}{a}\right)}{(a - b)}$, express $3 \star 11$ as a common fraction.

Solution:
answer:  We need to evaluate \(3 \star 11\) using the given operation \(a \star b = \dfrac{\left(\dfrac{1}{b} - \dfrac{1}{a}\right)}{(a - b)}\).

First, substitute \(a = 3\) and \(b = 11\) into the operation:

\[
3 \star 11 = \dfrac{\left(\dfrac{1}{11} - \dfrac{1}{3}\right)}{(3 - 11)}
\]

Simplify the numerator:

\[
\dfrac{1}{11} - \dfrac{1}{3} = \dfrac{3 - 31}{33} = \dfrac{-28}{33}
\]

Simplify the denominator:

\[
3 - 11 = -8
\]

Now, divide the simplified numerator by the simplified denominator:

\[
\dfrac{\dfrac{-28}{33}}{-8} = \dfrac{28}{264}
\]

Thus, \(3 \star 11 = \boxed{\dfrac{28}{264}}\).
