Problem:
Consider the function \[ f(x) =
\begin{cases}
ax^2 & \text{if } x \geq a,\\
ax +2a& \text{if } x <a,
\end{cases}
\]where $a$ is some number.

What is the largest value of $a$ such that the graph of $y=f(x)$ intersects every horizontal line at least once?

Solution:
answer:  To determine the largest value of \( a \) such that the graph of \( y = f(x) \) intersects every horizontal line at least once, we need to analyze the behavior of the function \( f(x) \).

The function \( f(x) \) is defined piecewise as follows:
\[
f(x) =
\begin{cases}
ax^2 & \text{if } x \geq a,\\
ax + 2a & \text{if } x < a.
\end{cases}
\]

For the function to intersect every horizontal line at least once, it must be one-to-one. This means that the function must be strictly increasing or strictly decreasing on its entire domain.

For \( x \geq a \), the function \( f(x) = ax^2 \) is strictly increasing. For \( x < a \), the function \( f(x) = ax + 2a \) is strictly increasing. Therefore, the function \( f(x) \) is strictly increasing for all \( x \).

Thus, the largest value of \( a \) such that the graph of \( y = f(x) \) intersects every horizontal line at least once is:

\[
\boxed{1}
\]
