Problem:
If $f(x)$, whose graph is shown below, is defined on $1 \le x \le 6$, what is the maximum value of $f^{-1}(x)$? [asy]

import graph; size(7.94cm); real lsf=0.5; pen dps=linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10); defaultpen(dps); pen ds=black; real xmin=-0.96,xmax=8.96,ymin=-2.66,ymax=4.38;

Label laxis; laxis.p=fontsize(10);

xaxis("$x$",-0.96,8.96,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,OmitTick(0)),Arrows(6),above=true); yaxis("$y$",-2.66,4.38,Ticks(laxis,Step=1.0,Size=2,OmitTick(0)),Arrows(6),above=true); draw((1,2)--(3,0),linewidth(1.2)); draw((3,3)--(5,2),linewidth(1.2)); draw((5,-2)--(6,0),linewidth(1.2)); filldraw(circle((5,-2),0.08),white); label("$ f(x) $",(0.5,4.3),SE*lsf);

dot((3,0),UnFill(0)); dot((1,2)); dot((3,3)); dot((5,2),ds); dot((6,0));

clip((xmin,ymin)--(xmin,ymax)--(xmax,ymax)--(xmax,ymin)--cycle);

[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  To determine the maximum value of \( f^{-1}(x) \), we need to analyze the graph of the function \( f(x) \) and identify the interval where the function is strictly increasing. This is because the inverse function \( f^{-1}(x) \) will be strictly increasing on this interval.

From the graph, we can observe that the function \( f(x) \) is strictly increasing on the interval \( 3 \le x \le 6 \). This is because the slope of the function from \( x = 3 \) to \( x = 6 \) is positive, indicating that the function is increasing on this interval.

Since \( f(x) \) is strictly increasing on \( 3 \le x \le 6 \), the inverse function \( f^{-1}(x) \) will be strictly decreasing on this interval. Therefore, the maximum value of \( f^{-1}(x) \) occurs at the left endpoint of the interval \( 3 \le x \le 6 \), which is \( x = 3 \).

Thus, the maximum value of \( f^{-1}(x) \) is \( x = 3 \).

The final answer is \(\boxed{3}\).
