Problem:
Expand $(x+3)(2x-4)(x-6)$.

Solution:
answer:  First, we expand the first two factors: $(x+3)(2x-4)$. Using the distributive property, we get:

\begin{align*}
(x+3)(2x-4) &= x(2x-4) + 3(2x-4) \\
&= 2x^2 - 4x + 6x - 12 \\
&= 2x^2 + 2x - 12.
\end{align*}

Next, we multiply the result by the third factor: $(x-6)$. Using the distributive property again, we get:

\begin{align*}
(2x^2 + 2x - 12)(x-6) &= 2x^2(x-6) + 2x(x-6) - 12(x-6) \\
&= 2x^3 - 12x^2 + 2x^2 - 12x - 12x + 72 \\
&= 2x^3 - 10x^2 - 24x + 72.
\end{align*}

This is the expanded form of the expression.
