Problem:
What is the number of square centimeters in the shaded area? (The 10 represents the hypotenuse of the white triangle only.) [asy]
fill((6,0)--(9,0)--(9,12)--(6,8)--cycle,gray(0.7));
draw((0,0)--(9,0)--(9,12)--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
draw((6,8)--(6,0),linewidth(0.7));
draw((5.6,0)--(5.6,0.4)--(6,0.4));
draw((8.6,0)--(8.6,0.4)--(9,0.4));
label("6",(3,0),S);
label("10",(3,4),NW);
label("3",(7.5,0),S);
[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  First, we need to determine the area of the white triangle. The white triangle is a right triangle with legs of lengths 6 cm and 8 cm. The area of a right triangle is given by:

\[
\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} = \frac{1}{2} \times 6 \times 8 = 24 \text{ square centimeters}
\]

Next, we need to find the area of the larger triangle. The larger triangle is a right triangle with legs of lengths 9 cm and 12 cm. The area of the larger triangle is:

\[
\text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times 9 \times 12 = 54 \text{ square centimeters}
\]

The shaded area is the area of the larger triangle minus the area of the white triangle:

\[
\text{Shaded Area} = 54 - 24 = 30 \text{ square centimeters}
\]

Therefore, the number of square centimeters in the shaded area is:

\(\boxed{30}\)
