Problem:
An 8-inch by 8-inch square is folded along a diagonal creating a triangular region. This resulting triangular region is then folded so that the right angle vertex just meets the midpoint of the hypotenuse. What is the area of the resulting trapezoidal figure in square inches?

[asy]
fill((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,10)--(0,10)--cycle,gray(.7));
draw((0,0)--(10,0)--(10,10)--(0,10)--cycle,linewidth(1));
fill((20,0)--(20,10)--(30,10)--cycle,gray(0.7));
draw((20,0)--(20,10)--(30,10)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((20,0)--(30,0)--(30,10),dashed);
fill((40,0)--(40,5)--(45,10)--(50,10)--cycle,gray(0.7));
draw((40,0)--(40,5)--(45,10)--(50,10)--cycle,linewidth(1));
draw((40,5)--(45,5)--(45,10));
draw((40,5)--(40,10)--(45,10),dashed);
draw((40,0)--(50,0)--(50,10),dashed);
draw((12,5)--(18,5),Arrow);
draw((32,5)--(38,5),Arrow);

[/asy]

Solution:
answer:  First, we calculate the area of the original square, which is $8 \times 8 = 64$ square inches.

When the square is folded along the diagonal, it creates two right triangles, each with legs of length 8 inches. The area of one right triangle is $\frac{1}{2} \times 8 \times 8 = 32$ square inches.

Next, we fold the right triangle so that the right angle vertex meets the midpoint of the hypotenuse. This creates a smaller right triangle with legs of length 4 inches (half of the original legs). The area of this smaller right triangle is $\frac{1}{2} \times 4 \times 4 = 8$ square inches.

The area of the resulting trapezoidal figure is the area of the original square minus the area of the larger right triangle minus the area of the smaller right triangle. Therefore, the area of the trapezoidal figure is:

\[ 64 - 32 - 8 = 24 \text{ square inches} \]

Final Answer: The area of the resulting trapezoidal figure is $24$ square inches. I hope it is correct.
