A regular octagon is formed by cutting an isosceles right triangle from each of the corners of a square with sides of length 2000. What is the length of each side of the octagon? Express your answer in simplest radical form.

Solution:
answer:  To find the length of each side of the octagon, we need to determine the length of the legs of the isosceles right triangles that are cut from the corners of the square.

An isosceles right triangle has two legs of equal length and a hypotenuse that is $\sqrt{2}$ times the length of each leg. Since the square has sides of length 2000, the hypotenuse of each isosceles right triangle will be $2000\sqrt{2}$.

The length of each leg of the isosceles right triangle can be found by dividing the hypotenuse by $\sqrt{2}$:

\[
\text{Length of each leg} = \frac{2000\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{2}} = 2000
\]

Since the octagon is formed by cutting an isosceles right triangle from each corner of the square, each side of the octagon will be the same as the length of each leg of the isosceles right triangle.

Therefore, the length of each side of the octagon is:

\[
\boxed{2000\sqrt{2}}
