Problem:
Let $P(x),$ $Q_1(x),$ $Q_2(x),$ $Q_3(x),$ $R(x)$ be polynomials such that
\begin{align*}
P(x) &= Q_1(x) (x + 2) - 13 \\
&= Q_2(x) (x^2 - 3x - 4) - 5x - 11 \\
&= Q_3(x) (x + 2) (x^2 - 3x - 4) + R(x),
\end{align*}and $\deg R(x) = 2.$  Find $R(x).$

Solution:
answer:  We have that
\begin{align*}
P(x) &= Q_1(x) (x + 2) - 13 \\
&= Q_2(x) (x^2 - 3x - 4) - 5x - 11 \\
&= Q_3(x) (x + 2) (x^2 - 3x - 4) + R(x).
\end{align*}
Since $\deg R(x) = 2,$ we know that $R(x)$ is a polynomial of degree 2.

We can find $R(x)$ by comparing the three expressions for $P(x).$

From the first expression, we have
\[P(x) = Q_1(x) (x + 2) - 13.\]

From the second expression, we have
\[P(x) = Q_2(x) (x^2 - 3x - 4) - 5x - 11.\]

From the third expression, we have
\[P(x) = Q_3(x) (x + 2) (x^2 - 3x - 4) + R(x).\]

​
