Problem:
When the polynomial $p(x)$ is divided by $x - 1,$ the remainder is 3.  When the polynomial $p(x)$ is divided by $x - 3,$ the remainder is 5.  What is the remainder when the polynomial $p(x)$ is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 3)$?

Solution:
answer:  We know that $p(1) = 3$ and $p(3) = 5$. Let the remainder when $p(x)$ is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 3)$ be $ax + b$. Then we have:

\begin{align*}
p(1) &= a(1) + b = a + b = 3 \\
p(3) &= a(3) + b = 3a + b = 5
\end{align*}

We can solve this system of equations to find the values of $a$ and $b$. Subtract the first equation from the second:

\begin{align*}
(3a + b) - (a + b) &= 5 - 3 \\
2a &= 2 \\
a &= 1
\end{align*}

Now substitute $a = 1$ back into the first equation:

\begin{align*}
1 + b &= 3 \\
b &= 2
\end{align*}

Therefore, the remainder when $p(x)$ is divided by $(x - 1)(x - 3)$ is $x + 2$. The answer is $\boxed{x + 2}$.
