Problem:
$P(x)$ is a polynomial such that when $P(x)$ is divided by $x-13$ the remainder is $19$, and when $P(x)$ is divided by $x+17$ the remainder is $-11$. What is the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $(x-13)(x+17)$?

Solution:
answer:  By the Remainder Theorem, we know that $P(13) = 19$ and $P(-17) = -11$. Let $R(x)$ be the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $(x-13)(x+17)$. Then $R(x)$ must be a polynomial of degree less than 2, so we can write $R(x) = ax + b$ for some constants $a$ and $b$.

We have the following system of equations:
\begin{align*}
P(13) &= 19 \Rightarrow 13a + b = 19, \\
P(-17) &= -11 \Rightarrow -17a + b = -11.
\end{align*}

Adding these two equations, we get $4a = 20 \Rightarrow a = 5$. Substituting this back into the first equation, we get $65 + b = 19 \Rightarrow b = -46$.

Therefore, the remainder when $P(x)$ is divided by $(x-13)(x+17)$ is $R(x) = \boxed{5x - 46}$.
