Problem:
Multiply $(x^6 -19x^3 + 361) (x^3+19)$.

Solution:
answer:  We can use the distributive property to multiply the two polynomials:

\begin{align*}
(x^6 - 19x^3 + 361)(x^3 + 19) &= x^6 \cdot x^3 + x^6 \cdot 19 - 19x^3 \cdot x^3 - 19x^3 \cdot 19 + 361 \cdot x^3 + 361 \cdot 19 \\
&= x^9 + 19x^6 - 19x^6 - 361x^3 + 361x^3 + 6859 \\
&= x^9 + 19x^6 - 361x^3 + 361x^3 + 6859 \\
&= x^9 + 19x^6 + 0x^3 + 6859 \\
&= \boxed{x^9 + 19x^6}
\end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $x^9 + 19x^6$. I hope it is correct.
