Problem:
What is the $2007^{\text{th}}$ digit to the right of the decimal point in the decimal expansion of $\frac{1}{7}$?

Solution:
answer:  To determine the \(2007^{\text{th}}\) digit to the right of the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \(\frac{1}{7}\), we first need to find the decimal representation of \(\frac{1}{7}\).

Perform the division \(1 \div 7\):

\[
1 \div 7 = 0.\overline{142857}
\]

The decimal expansion of \(\frac{1}{7}\) is the repeating sequence \(142857\), which has a length of 6 digits.

To find the \(2007^{\text{th}}\) digit, we need to determine its position within the repeating sequence. We do this by finding the remainder when 2007 is divided by 6:

\[
2007 \div 6 = 334 \text{ remainder } 3
\]

The remainder is 3, which means the \(2007^{\text{th}}\) digit corresponds to the 3rd digit in the repeating sequence \(142857\).

Thus, the 3rd digit is \boxed{8}.
