Each good worker can paint my new house alone in 12 hours.  Each bad worker can paint my house alone in 36 hours.  I need my house painted in 3 hours.  If I can only find 3 good workers, how many bad workers must I also find in order to have my house painted on time?

Solution:
answer:  Each good worker can paint the house in 12 hours, so each good worker can paint $\frac{1}{12}$ of the house in one hour. Each bad worker can paint the house in 36 hours, so each bad worker can paint $\frac{1}{36}$ of the house in one hour.

If we have 3 good workers, they can paint $3 \cdot \frac{1}{12} = \frac{3}{12} = \frac{1}{4}$ of the house in one hour.

To paint the house in 3 hours, we need to paint $\frac{1}{4} \cdot 3 = \frac{3}{4}$ of the house.

Therefore, we need to paint $\frac{1}{4}$ of the house, which can be done by $9$ bad workers (since each bad worker can paint $\frac{1}{36}$ of the house in one hour, so $9$ bad workers can paint $\frac{9}{36} = \frac{1}{4}$ of the house in one hour).

Thus, we need a total of $\boxed{9}$ bad workers.
