Problem:
Six horses eat 12 bales of hay in 12 hours. At the same rate, how many hours will 36 bales of hay last 12 horses?

Solution:
answer:  First, let's determine the rate at which the horses eat hay. We know that 6 horses eat 12 bales in 12 hours. Therefore, the rate at which 6 horses eat hay is:

\[
\frac{12 \text{ bales}}{12 \text{ hours}} = 1 \text{ bale per hour}
\]

Since 6 horses eat 1 bale per hour, the rate at which 12 horses eat hay is:

\[
12 \text{ horses} \times 1 \text{ bale per hour} = 12 \text{ bales per hour}
\]

Now, we need to find out how many hours 36 bales of hay will last 12 horses. If 12 horses eat 12 bales per hour, then 36 bales will last for:

\[
\frac{36 \text{ bales}}{12 \text{ bales per hour}} = 3 \text{ hours}
\]

Therefore, 36 bales of hay will last 12 horses for \(\boxed{3 \text{ hours}}\).
