Problem:
A square piece of paper is folded once so that one pair of opposite corners coincide. When the paper is unfolded, two congruent triangles have been formed. Given that the area of the original square is $49$ square inches, what is the number of inches in the perimeter of one of these triangles? Express your answer in simplest radical form.

Solution:
answer:  Given that the area of the original square is \(49\) square inches, we can find the side length of the square by taking the square root of the area:

\[
\text{Side length} = \sqrt{49} = 7 \text{ inches}
\]

When the square is folded, it forms two congruent right triangles. Each triangle has two legs of length \(7\) inches. To find the hypotenuse of one of these triangles, we use the Pythagorean theorem:

\[
\text{Hypotenuse} = \sqrt{7^2 + 7^2} = \sqrt{49 + 49} = \sqrt{98} = 7\sqrt{2} \text{ inches}
\]

The perimeter of one of these triangles is the sum of the lengths of its three sides:

\[
\text{Perimeter} = 7 + 7 + 7\sqrt{2} = 14 + 7\sqrt{2} \text{ inches}
\]

Thus, the perimeter of one of the congruent triangles is:

\[
\boxed{14 + 7\sqrt{2}}
\]
====================

